                "content": "**(1)  Perturbation matrix in the ordered basis  \n\\((|2s\\rangle ,|2p_{z}\\rangle ,|2p_{y}\\rangle ,|2p_{x}\\rangle )\\)**  \n\nThe perturbation is  \n\n\\[\nH' = eEz+\\frac{\\mu _B B}{\\hbar }\\,L_{x}.\n\\]\n\nOnly the following matrix elements are non\u2011vanishing (the rest are zero by symmetry):\n\n* **Stark term** \u2013 only between \\(2s\\) and \\(2p_{z}\\)\n\n\\[\n\\langle 2s|eEz|2p_{z}\\rangle = d,\\qquad \nd\\equiv 3ea_{0}E .\n\\]\n\n* **Orbital\u2011Zeeman term** \u2013 \\(L_{x}\\) changes the magnetic quantum number by \\(\\Delta m=\\pm 1\\).  \nIn the real\u2011orbital basis  \n\n\\[\n|2p_{x}\\rangle =\\frac{|m=+1\\rangle+|m=-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt 2},\n\\qquad   \n|2p_{z}\\rangle =|m=0\\rangle ,\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\n\\langle 2p_{x}|L_{x}|2p_{z}\\rangle =\\hbar ,\\qquad\n\\langle 2p_{z}|L_{x}|2p_{x}\\rangle =\\hbar .\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\langle 2p_{x}|H'_{\\rm Z}|2p_{z}\\rangle=\n\\frac{\\mu _B B}{\\hbar }\\,\\hbar =\\mu _B B,\n\\qquad\n\\langle 2p_{z}|H'_{\\rm Z}|2p_{x}\\rangle =\\mu _B B .\n\\]\n\nAll other matrix elements of \\(L_{x}\\) inside the \\((2p_{y},2p_{x})\\)\u2013\\(2p_{z}\\) subspace vanish.\n\nPutting everything together,\n\n\\[\nH'=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & d & 0 & 0\\\\[2pt]\nd & 0 & 0 & \\mu _B B\\\\[2pt]\n0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\mu _B B & 0 & 0\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\text{ordered as }(|2s\\rangle ,|2p_{z}\\rangle ,|2p_{y}\\rangle ,|2p_{x}\\rangle ).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**(2)  First\u2011order energy shifts and their degeneracies**\n\nThe matrix consists of three blocks:\n\n* a \\(1\\times1\\) block for \\(|2p_{y}\\rangle\\) (zero);\n* a \\(2\\times2\\) block for \\(|2s\\rangle\\)\u2013\\(|2p_{z}\\rangle\\)\u2013\\(|2p_{x}\\rangle\\) :\n\n\\[\nM=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & d & 0\\\\\nd & 0 & \\mu _B B\\\\\n0 & \\mu _B B & 0\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe characteristic polynomial of \\(M\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\det (\\lambda I-M)=\\lambda\\bigl(\\lambda^{2}-\\mu _B^{2}B^{2}-d^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the eigenvalues (first\u2011order shifts) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\lambda_{1}=0,\\qquad \n\\lambda_{2}=+\\sqrt{d^{2}+(\\mu _B B)^{2}},\\qquad \n\\lambda_{3}=-\\sqrt{d^{2}+(\\mu _B B)^{2}}\\;}\n\\]\n\ntogether with the isolated zero coming from \\(|2p_{y}\\rangle\\).\n\nConsequently the spectrum of the \\(n=2\\) manifold is  \n\n| shift \\(\\Delta E\\) | degeneracy |\n|-------------------|------------|\n| \\(0\\)             | 2 (states \\(|2p_{y}\\rangle\\) and the appropriate linear combination of \\(|2s\\rangle,|2p_{z}\\rangle,|2p_{x}\\rangle\\)) |\n| \\(+\\sqrt{d^{2}+(\\mu _B B)^{2}}\\) | 1 |\n| \\(-\\sqrt{d^{2}+(\\mu _B B)^{2}}\\) | 1 |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**(3)  Check of the limiting cases**\n\n* **Electric field only (\\(B=0\\)).**  \n  Then \\(\\mu _B B=0\\) and the eigenvalues reduce to  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Delta E =0\\;( {\\rm doubly\\;degenerate}),\\qquad \n  \\Delta E =\\pm d =\\pm 3ea_{0}E .\n  \\]\n\n  This is precisely the linear Stark effect for the \\(n=2\\) level: the\n  \\(|2s\\rangle\\)\u2013\\(|2p_{z}\\rangle\\) mixing produces the two shifted\n  components, while \\(|2p_{x}\\rangle\\) and \\(|2p_{y}\\rangle\\) stay at the\n  unperturbed energy.\n\n* **Magnetic field only (\\(E=0\\)).**  \n  Now \\(d=0\\) and the eigenvalues become  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Delta E =0\\;( {\\rm doubly\\;degenerate}),\\qquad \n  \\Delta E =\\pm \\mu _B B .\n  \\]\n\n  These are exactly the orbital Zeeman splittings for an \\(l=1\\) electron:\n  the state with \\(m=0\\) stays at zero, while the combination\n  \\(|2p_{x}\\rangle\\) (which contains \\(m=\\pm1\\)) acquires the shifts\n  \\(\\pm\\mu _B B\\).\n\nThus the matrix correctly reproduces the familiar separate effects.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**(4)  Why the shifts cannot be added independently**\n\nWhen both fields are present the perturbation matrix is **not diagonal**\nin the basis that diagonalises either the Stark or the Zeeman part\nalone.  The off\u2011diagonal element \\(d\\) couples \\(|2s\\rangle\\) to\n\\(|2p_{z}\\rangle\\); the off\u2011diagonal element \\(\\mu _B B\\) couples\n\\(|2p_{z}\\rangle\\) to \\(|2p_{x}\\rangle\\).  Consequently the true\neigenstates are linear combinations of these three states, and the\ncorresponding energies are the eigenvalues of the whole \\(3\\times3\\)\nmatrix, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\pm\\sqrt{d^{2}+(\\mu _B B)^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nIf one were to \u201cadd\u201d the Stark shift \\(\\pm d\\) to the Zeeman shift\n\\(\\pm\\mu _B B\\) one would obtain four possible values\n\\(\\pm d\\pm\\mu _B B\\), which do **not** coincide with the actual\nsplittings.  The reason is that the two perturbations act **coherently**\non the same set of states; the resulting energy shift is the magnitude\nof the resultant vector in the \\((d,\\mu _B B)\\) plane, not the algebraic\nsum of the individual components.  Only after diagonalising the full\nperturbation Hamiltonian does one obtain the correct first\u2011order energy\nshifts.",
